WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Hi. So, my goal today is to try and share a little bit of what I've learned about working on the tidyverse for the last 20 years

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:21.000
and then try and speculate a little bit about what of those things might still hold true today.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:33.000
Because of course, you know, the elephant in the room is AI, who's going to try and squeeze right past us.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:42.000
And this really, it does feel tough because it feels like AI has kind of come along and just torn up many of the playbooks that we've had,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
whether it's for the career, or reaching out to the community, or programming, or open source.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:56.000
It's really hard to tell, like, what's still going to remain relevant over the next, you know, the next 20 years.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.000
So I'm going to start by talking a little bit about the tidyverse in case you haven't heard of it.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.000
How many of you here have used the tidyverse? Okay, well we'll go pretty quickly then.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:15.000
So, if you've forgotten, the tidyverse is, the goal of the tidyverse is to create a language that you can use to solve data science challenges

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.000
and with R code, so it's fundamentally tied to the R language.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.000
And it's really connected to my model of data science, which starts with importing your data,

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.000
then doing some tidying to get it into a form that's amenable for further work,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
and then you get into the loop of understanding, of visualizing, of transforming, of modeling your data,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
then you're going to conclude with communicating that work to someone else.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.000
And all of this work you are doing in a programming language.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:51.000
You're not pointing and clicking, you're typing, you're communicating to the computer with code what you wanted to do.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.000
And one of the goals of the tidyverse was to help you not get to like a peak of success,

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:06.000
where you have to struggle the whole way, but to help you kind of fall into the pit of success.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.000
So hopefully, if you do not know, OAP is what Minnesotans say when they have an accident.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:20.000
And this is very much in line with the vision of John Chambers, who created S, the language that led to R,

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.000
that really the goal is to take users, to allow them to start in an interactive environment,

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.000
and to kind of gradually like just slide into programming, without really kind of knowing it,

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:39.000
or learning too much about it, but eventually over time, they become more and more sophisticated programmers.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.000
And it seems to have worked.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:53.000
So what I kind of come up with is three things that I think have mattered to the tidyverse over the last 20 years,

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:58.000
and will hopefully continue to matter for a long time to come.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.000
So abstractions, tools, and people.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:08.000
And so for each of these, my goal is to give you a few of the reasons why I think they have been important,

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.000
to kind of speculate on why I think they will continue to be important,

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:21.000
and then to kind of give a few pointers to my work and some of the other folks at Posit along these lines leading into the future.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:31.000
So I think, like when I think today, like if I was going to sit down and teach someone data science,

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.000
like what would I teach them?

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.000
I, you know, I still love programming.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.000
I still love to produce code.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:48.000
I pretty strongly believe that reading code is still really important, particularly if you're doing data science.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.000
But it's clear that like writing, the ability to write code is much less important than it's ever been.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.000
But what is still important?

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.000
I think some of these abstractions are really useful to know.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.000
So when I kind of look back at my work, you know, I think one of the really important things is like thinking about tidy data.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Like this idea that it's really useful to get your data into a form where every column is a variable and every row is an observation.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:23.000
It's probably like easy now to not have tidy data because a coding agent will just grind away at the problem.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:33.000
It will solve it using like whatever horrendous, awful code it has to do to work with whatever structure your data is currently in.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:41.000
But I also believe that if you up front say, hey, make sure my data is tidy, you're going to end up with code that is easier to understand.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:47.000
It's going to be easier for the agent to write, and it's going to be easier for you to verify that it's correct.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:05:03.000
So I think the principles of tidy data is still really, really important, even if a lot of the knowledge of like how do I tidy this specific data set maybe is less important because an agent might be able to figure that out today.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:08.000
The other thing that I think about is about ggplot2 and the grammar of graphics.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:22.000
And certainly today like does it matter if you know the theme syntax for ggplot2 so you can control the size of the font or the number of ticks on an axis or the angle at which those ticks are?

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:30.000
That's probably, that's now I think safely information that you can just allow to fall out of your brain and an agent to take over.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:47.000
But the fundamental idea of the grammar of graphics, I think that is still so incredibly powerful because it gives you a cognitive framework for understanding visualizations, and it gives you a precise technical vocabulary to talk about them.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:56.000
And I think that's going to pay off regardless of whether you're talking to another human, formulating the thoughts in your head, or communicating them to an agent.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:12.000
And so to me like the grammar of graphics, like the core idea is when you produce a visualization, that's fundamentally a mapping from things in your data, variables in your data, to aesthetic properties that you can perceive like color and shape and size.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:21.000
That's mediated by a scale, that's what controls the details of going from a number to a color or an enum to a shape.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:38.000
It's rendered by a geometric mark like a point or a line or a rectangle, optionally transformed by some statistics, maybe you're doing a histogram, maybe you're doing a box plot, you're not displaying the raw data anymore, you're displaying some distribution or summary.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:54.000
And finally, all of that's going to be laid out on a coordinate system, typically Cartesian, but also very commonly if you're working with a map, you don't want to use a Cartesian coordinate system, or if you are working in polar coordinates, which is often also very useful.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:10.000
So I believe those like, those abstractions are still powerful and important, because they give you a way to break up these like big thorny problems into smaller self-contained pieces.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:19.000
When you have those abstractions, you have a better ability to think about a problem, and you have a better ability to communicate that to an agent.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:32.000
So abstractions are important, and I think one of the things that's exciting to me is that AI gives us the opportunity to bring those abstractions to new audiences.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:44.000
So one thing that my team has been working on recently, Thomas Peterson, Tian Vandenbrand, and George Stack have been working on is GGSQL.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:50.000
And if you know what ggplot2 is, and you know what SQL is, you can basically guess what ggsql is.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:54.000
It's a SQL-inspired syntax for the grammar of graphics.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.000
So this is what ggsql looks like.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:01.000
It starts out with some, you know, regular SQL code.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:05.000
You're going to generate a data set, and then you're going to visualize it.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:12.000
Here we're mapping the build length to the x position, the build depth to the y position, the body map to the fill color.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.000
We're going to visualize that using a point.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:25.000
We're also going to add on top of that an annotation, a line that shows us a slope of 0.4 with a y-intercept of negative 1.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:34.000
We're going to map the fill body mass to the fill color using a bin scale, and then we're going to add some labels.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:40.000
And then we run that with ggsql, we get something that looks like a very classical ggplot2 graphic,

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:46.000
but now we have something that's 100% accessible to someone that only works in SQL.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:55.000
And I'm pretty excited about this, because I think the grammar of graphics is an incredibly powerful framework for thinking about visualization,

00:08:55.000 --> 00:09:00.000
and this is not something that SQL users have had access to in the past.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:09.000
The other reason we've been working on ggsql is it's also sometimes nice to generate a visualization in a language that's kind of guaranteed to be safe.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:17.000
If you're generating R code to create ggplot2 or Python code to create matplotlib, that code can do anything.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:22.000
Hopefully it's only going to create the plot, but it could be doing whatever to your computer.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:28.000
Nice thing about SQL, you can run this and you can know this isn't going to go and delete files,

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:33.000
it's not going to read your passwords and email them to maybe Grok.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.000
Fairly newsworthy.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.000
Okay, so I think abstractions have mattered and continue to matter.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:45.000
The other thing, the next thing that I think really matters are tools.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:54.000
And for me, tools have always been this lever that has allowed me to have a much bigger impact than I could without them.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:10:01.000
And when I think about the sort of tools that we have worked on, this is not just the tools for data science,

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:11.000
but we very quickly got to a point where we were developing so many R packages that what we really needed were tools to help us develop R packages.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:19.000
And so this is what led to the development of a lot of the key kind of software engineering infrastructure for R,

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:26.000
like TestThat for unit testing, Roxygen2 for documenting your package, DevTools,

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:33.000
which makes it as easy as possible to work with kind of a development version of a package rather than having to install it all the time.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:40.000
And then most recently, Packagedown, which turns your package documentation into a beautiful website.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:48.000
So many, I mean, I think pretty much all of these things you can take for granted with Python.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:52.000
Python has had these for much longer than R ever did.

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:58.000
But these were some of the tools that we realized we needed these software engineering tools in R.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:04.000
And they, you know, fundamentally accelerated the pace of development because with unit tests, you know,

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:07.000
we could be much more confident that the code was actually correct.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:13.000
And when fixing bug A, we didn't accidentally produce bug B.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:20.000
And I'll talk more about this later, but like the goal of software, I guess at least traditionally,

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:24.000
has been for people to use it, for humans to use it.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:29.000
And humans need not just to see the code, but they need to understand what it does.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.000
They need good documentation.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:37.000
They need aesthetically pleasing websites that they enjoy browsing.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:40.000
But to me, tools aren't just code.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:44.000
Like another set of tools we have are checklists.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:51.000
Like I think checklists are an incredibly, incredibly powerful tool when you cannot fully automate something.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:56.000
So this is the checklist, which I see is very difficult for you to see.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:57.000
It doesn't really matter.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:03.000
But this is the checklist that we follow before we release a new version of an R package.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:06.000
So this is something that we have tooled up.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:11.000
We have an R function that creates a GitHub issue that has all these checkboxes

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:20.000
that force you to work through our standard release process that encourages you to follow all of the best practices

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:23.000
that we've discovered over the last 20 years.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:30.000
One of the maybe surprising things that's part of our release process for major releases, minor releases,

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.000
is writing a blog post.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:38.000
You are not allowed to release your package until you have written a blog post.

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:44.000
And this is because one of our very hard-won learnings was that very reliably when you write a blog post,

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:51.000
that's when you discover a bug that you haven't discovered the whole other time of developing the package.

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:56.000
So we now always do the blog post before we actually do the test.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:01.000
And of course we have like a fundamental new tool now, and that's Cloud Code.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:07.000
And I have found Cloud Code to be like tremendously kind of fun and empowering.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:12.000
And here for a few packages that I've kind of been noodling on recently,

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:17.000
we've got on the y-axis the number of open issues, the x-axis is time,

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:26.000
and you can see the points at which I have like decided to attack one of these packages with me and with Cloud Code.

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:31.000
So I've always been a very efficient issue closer.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:38.000
I say that not necessarily an efficient bug fixer, but I am very good at closing issues quickly.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:44.000
I think Cloud has made me even faster at closing issues, but it's also increased the quality.

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:48.000
Like now I actually fix more of those bugs rather than declaring them as won't fix.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:56.000
But I think you can see from this, like now I can fairly reliably in two weeks to a month close 100 to 150 issues.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:03.000
And this is me doing this kind of as part of my regular job, squeezing it in between meetings.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:09.000
This has really like fundamentally accelerated my R package development experience.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:15.000
And I don't know if you can see this, but the kind of magic line for me is 25 issues,

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:20.000
because once you get below 25 issues, they all fit on a single page and get up.

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:24.000
And I think that's a thing of beauty.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:29.000
I've also been using this new tool, Cloud Tools, to make other cool tools.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:36.000
So I have this package called Bananarama, which is what I use to generate all of the images in this presentation.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:41.000
It's a YAML specification that allows me to declare a common style for all of the images

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:44.000
and then allows me to say, hey, this is what I want.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:46.000
Go away and draw it.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:50.000
And the thing that's particularly important is it allows you to kind of anchor.

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:54.000
This is just a Gemini nano banana feature.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:15:02.000
But you can anchor the prompts with images, increasing the consistency across the images in your presentation.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:07.000
And this feels like this feels like really fun to me.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:13.000
Like, I am just having a great old time, like, ripping through issues in R packages.

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:19.000
But at the same time, it also kind of feels like, well, when you kind of look at the outside world,

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:24.000
like, well, there's a lot of, like, crazy shit going on out there.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:29.000
And is it really the best use of my time to be fixing bugs in these R packages?

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:34.000
So while I love doing that, I've also been trying to force myself to, like,

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:40.000
engage more in the fear and anxiety that AI brings to pretty much all of us.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:45.000
And so I want to talk about a new tool that I've been working on.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:54.000
I think this is the I guess this is the first tool I've ever developed in at least 20 years that is not explicitly about,

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:59.000
oh, this is a cross-platform, cross-language tool like everyone these days.

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:05.000
Of course, I am writing a standalone command line interface in Rust.

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:10.000
But the idea here is to come up with a new specification for data dictionaries.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:14.000
You know, people have tried to do this many times over the last years.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:16.000
But I think it's time to take another look at it.

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:22.000
Because I think it is so important now to encourage people to write down what they know about data.

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:26.000
Because if you haven't written it down, your agents aren't going to know about it.

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:28.000
And, you know, plot twist.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.000
Actually, this is going to help your human colleagues as well.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.000
Isn't that nice?

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:36.000
So the basic idea of DataDict is, like, very, very simple.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:42.000
You're going to declare, like, for each of the tables in your data set, for each of the columns, like, what are they?

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.000
That's a string.

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:46.000
Oh, this is the primary key.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:52.000
And we're going to have some free text description where you can include whatever you know, whatever weirdness and oddities.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:54.000
And we're also going to include a few examples.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:59.000
Because examples are so useful for both humans and AI agents.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:04.000
And then DataDict.yaml is coupled, as I said, with a command line interface.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:09.000
That's going to allow you to validate your spec, to validate the data.

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:16.000
Using some of the really cool tools in Rust for producing beautiful, informative error messages.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:19.000
That allow you to see exactly what's gone wrong and why.

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:27.000
And the other thing that's really cool with command line CLIs these days is you can make them completely self-documenting.

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:31.000
So that if an agent wants to use this, it's going to call DataDict.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:34.000
It's going to see, oh, actually, there's a couple of skills bundled here.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:38.000
And then it can read those skills and learn how to use DataDict.

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:45.000
So I think one of my, like, one of my early fears when AI was really picking up was, like, how?

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:48.000
Like, what's going to, like, this is great for ggplot2, right?

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:51.000
There's, like, 20 years of ggplot2 code on the internet.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:52.000
Great for me.

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:55.000
What's going to happen with the next generation of tools?

00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:59.000
How is AI going to know how to generate their code?

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:07.000
And I think what we're learning now, the way we deal with that, is if you're producing a new tool today, that tool needs to be self-documenting.

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:12.000
And, like, in many ways, that's great for agents, but that's great for humans, too, right?

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:21.000
Great to bundle everything you need to learn the tool that you're trying to use.

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:29.000
And the last thing that matters, maybe the most important thing that matters, are people.

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:35.000
And this has always been, like, front of mind to me.

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:38.000
Because if you build it, like, they won't come.

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:47.000
Like, you can make the best tool in the world, and if you don't tell anyone about it, like, they're not going to use it.

00:18:47.000 --> 00:19:00.000
And I think this is something that, like, folks in academia often feel kind of uncomfortable with, this idea of, like, tooting your own horn or, like, self-promotion.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:06.000
But seriously, if you don't tell people about the awesome work you're doing, they're never going to learn about it.

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:14.000
Like, your impact on the world is a product of the quality of the work and the quantity of people that are using that work.

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:21.000
And I think you almost have an obligation, if you think you're doing good work, to tell people about it.

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:25.000
So I've always believed that, like, marketing is really important.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:33.000
One of the cool things, the things I loved about the R community is our culture of hex stickers.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:40.000
These are, first of all, they are infinitely better than Python stickers, because they tile the plane beautifully.

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:49.000
You can put a bunch of stickers on your laptop, and they don't have to overlap each other, which is a crime against nature.

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:55.000
And it's just such an amazing way for people to kind of express their personality and their humanity.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:20:04.000
So many kind of great conversations, connections that have been kicked off because someone sees, oh, hey, I saw on your laptop that you used this package.

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:05.000
I used that package, too.

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:08.000
That's so cool.

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:13.000
So that kind of community connection, I think, is super-duper important, too.

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:22.000
We've also spent a lot of time working on cheat sheets, making it as easy as possible for people to learn a new tool.

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:28.000
But very, very obviously, like, the way that community works has changed.

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:36.000
So this is a plot of normalized stack overflow activity, answers, comments, questions, and votes.

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:41.000
And you can see that basically stack overflow, like, it's dead.

00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:45.000
Stack overflow is dead, and AI killed it.

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:56.000
And I think that, like, of all of these things that matter, I don't really know what's going to happen with people, and I don't have any particularly great solutions.

00:20:56.000 --> 00:21:02.000
But I just want to acknowledge that there is this massive change that's happened.

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:06.000
Like, was stack overflow the best community?

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:07.000
No.

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:09.000
Was it the friendliest community?

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.000
Absolutely not.

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:22.000
Although I will say, like, compared to me growing up in the R help mailing list, the stack overflow was infinitely more friendly than that mailing list, if you can imagine it.

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:29.000
So, like, I don't know what's going to happen, but we're going to need to create new mechanisms for community.

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:44.000
I do get the sense that we're sort of starting to, I think, recover from COVID slowly, that there's an increasing kind of desperate need for personal connection, particularly as more and more of us are just interacting with AI all day.

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:53.000
I think there's, like, a growing need to connect with other humans, to share, like, our fears and anxieties and hopes and excitements about the future.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:22:01.000
So, this is not doom and gloom, but this is, like, we're going to need to create new mechanisms to bring people together.

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:20.000
So, to kind of sum up, you know, the three things that I think have always mattered and will continue to always matter, abstractions, like, breaking down complicated problems into simple primitives that help us understand them, that help us write code to describe them, super, super important.

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:24.000
The tools we use, really important.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:39.000
I guess the counter to that is also to not, tools can be intoxicating, too, and you have to always make sure you're stepping back and saying, like, am I having fun or am I doing, like, the right thing, the most important thing?

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:43.000
And then, finally, people are the most important thing.

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:52.000
We don't know, like, the way communities operate has had a fundamental tectonic, irrevocable tectonic shift.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:58.000
We are going to need to figure out how to deal with that over the next five to ten years.

00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:27.000
And then just the very last slide, you know, I just want to make sure that I express, like, I feel both, like, you know, fundamental existential dread about AI, like, what does it mean for me, for my identity as a data scientist, as a software engineer, as a community builder, and this, like, excitement of this empowerment, you know, like, I vibe coded this talk timer iPhone app, which I never could have done before.

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:34.000
This, and I don't, there's no resolving, there's no unifying these feelings.

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:41.000
All you can do is kind of accept that you're going to feel these tensions pulling you in both directions.

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:53.000
There's nothing you can do about that except accept it and, you know, try and take a deep breath and find other people to share those experiences with.

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:54.000
Thank you.

00:23:54.000 --> 00:24:04.000
Thank you.

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:15.000
And while we take questions, one of my unpaid interns is going to distribute 3D printed snakes, if anyone would like a 3D printed snake.

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:16.000
Thank you, Hadley.

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:19.000
We have a few questions on Slack.

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:22.000
The first one is from Henry Schreiner.

00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:26.000
Can you convert this data dictionary to JSON schema?

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:30.000
It seems similar enough that it could be a mechanical translation from that.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:44.000
Yes, so one of the reasons for choosing, I think, YAML, particularly YAML 1.2, if you really want to get technical, is the sweet spot for a file format that is machine readable, is human readable, and agent readable.

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:45.000
Absolutely.

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:49.000
It is, you know, there's a one-to-one correspondence with some JSON document.

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:57.000
Making people write JSON by hand is cruel and unusual punishment.

00:24:57.000 --> 00:24:59.000
Question from Thomas Caswell.

00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:02.000
Will mailing list make a comeback?

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:05.000
Discourse instances, real-time chat only?

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:08.000
Yeah, I don't know.

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:12.000
I mean, I guess this is an appropriate time for me to plug.

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:16.000
Actually, I don't know how to link to my...

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:17.000
I don't even know how to show you.

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:19.000
It's called...

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:22.000
What is it even called?

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:24.000
I created this at an early age.

00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:25.000
But, like, I don't know.

00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:30.000
Like, this is one of my experiments that, like, yeah, I actually like...

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:35.000
It feels weird to say this, but I actually like people sending me emails now.

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:38.000
Like, it's kind of nice to get a long-form email that I can read.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:40.000
So I'm experimenting doing with this.

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:45.000
I'm trying to explain AI as much as I know about it as we go.

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:48.000
But, yeah, like, I think maybe mailing list will make a comeback.

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:49.000
Maybe discourse.

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:55.000
I'm kind of hopeful that maybe it's time to bring the in-person meetup back.

00:25:55.000 --> 00:26:02.000
Because I know that that in-person connection, again, is just so valuable and so satisfying.

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:07.000
Well, this is a big in-person meetup.

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:12.000
And plus one on that.

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:15.000
A question from Christian.

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:24.000
Are you worried about how we will learn and discover new things that matter when cognitive outsourcing to AI is so available?

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:30.000
A lot of most productive AI users are leveraging their pre-existing skills when using AI.

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:32.000
Yeah, absolutely.

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:37.000
I think it's, like, to me, it's very clear, like, from plots.

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:39.000
I can't even find the spot again.

00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:43.000
From, like, the fact of, like, how...

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:44.000
Like, this plot.

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:47.000
Like, I am a massive winner from AI.

00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:49.000
Because I have 20 years of experience.

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:55.000
I can very, very quickly look at a PR and gut check whether this is correct or not.

00:26:55.000 --> 00:26:58.000
Like, what does this mean for the next generation?

00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:07.000
How the heck are they going to learn when there's just such this tempting siren of, like, I can just have AI do that for me?

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:08.000
I...

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:10.000
Yeah, it's scary.

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:15.000
I am hopeful on the whole that I think this is a problem we can solve.

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:18.000
Like, we need to discover new ways of doing this.

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:19.000
Doing things.

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:25.000
It feels like maybe, like, maybe everyone should have one AI-free day a week.

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:29.000
Where you can deliberately turn off your AI and do things like the old-fashioned way.

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:31.000
To practice those old skills.

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:34.000
But I'm also conscious of, like...

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:36.000
Like, I don't want to be the guy that's, like...

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:42.000
You know, back in my day, I had to walk to school in bare feet, in the snow, uphill both ways.

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:46.000
And now you're gonna have to do that, too, just because I suffered.

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:47.000
I'm gonna make you suffer.

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:53.000
Like, I very strongly believe that, like, I suffered so you did not have to suffer.

00:27:54.000 --> 00:28:03.000
And I always worry where I hear myself saying, like, the things that my curmudgeonly old professors said to me back in the day.

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:10.000
That, like, we also don't need to make people, like, retrace our footsteps of learning.

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:13.000
We can discover new ways for people to learn.

00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:20.000
Maybe this audience will get one of my somewhat abstractly related quotes to this.

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:25.000
But, like, ontogeny does not recapitulate phylogeny.

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:30.000
Like, when you look at an embryo developing, it kind of looks like it's going through all these stages.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:33.000
Like, it looks a little bit like a lizard and it looks like a bird.

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:34.000
But that's not really what's happening.

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:39.000
And we don't have to make people go through all of the same painful stages we did.

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:42.000
We can figure out how to skip that and still have people learn.

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:45.000
Thank you.

00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:47.000
And one more question.

00:28:47.000 --> 00:28:51.000
I don't want you to miss your break.

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:54.000
A question from Ken Williams.

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:55.000
Great talk.

00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:59.000
What is the difference between documented and self-documented?

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:05.000
There is no difference, I guess.

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:11.000
Yeah, I guess I don't really understand that question.

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:13.000
As long as you've written it down, you've documented it.

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:17.000
If you share that with other people, that's the whole point of documentation.

